WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.928
Secure input handling depends on what happens when input is wrong.

2
00:00:04.928 --> 00:00:13.439
Fail closed means the program rejects, stops, or falls back to a safe state when a required check fails.

3
00:00:13.439 --> 00:00:17.023
It does not continue with partially initialized data.

4
00:00:17.023 --> 00:00:23.295
It does not guess a default that changes the security meaning of the request.

5
00:00:23.295 --> 00:00:29.119
It does not log an error and keep processing as if validation succeeded.

6
00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:37.631
Consistent error returns make this easier because callers can handle one clear failure path instead of many ambiguous outcomes.

7
00:00:37.631 --> 00:00:41.662
Cleanup paths deserve the same attention as success paths.

8
00:00:41.662 --> 00:00:50.622
A parser that allocates memory, opens files, locks resources, or builds partial objects must release them correctly when validation fails.

9
00:00:50.622 --> 00:00:55.550
The cleanup code should avoid leaks, double frees, and use-after-free behavior.

10
00:00:55.550 --> 00:01:03.613
Logging is useful, but log messages should not expose secrets, raw tokens, private paths, or unbounded attacker-controlled text.

11
00:01:03.613 --> 00:01:09.437
Error messages should help operators diagnose problems without becoming another input handling problem.

12
00:01:09.437 --> 00:01:25.117
Testing should include the inputs real programs actually receive: empty input, maximum-length input, one byte over the limit, missing terminators, malformed encodings, invalid numbers, huge counts, negative values, truncated records, duplicate fields, and random data.

13
00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:28.700
Unit tests are good for known boundaries.

14
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:35.420
Fuzzing is valuable because it explores combinations humans do not think to write by hand.

15
00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:45.276
Compiler warnings, sanitizers, and static analysis add another layer by surfacing memory errors, undefined behavior, suspicious conversions, and boundary mistakes during development.

16
00:01:45.276 --> 00:01:52.444
The point of testing malformed input is to prove that rejection is as engineered as acceptance.
